        ],
        "summary": "It runs in the checkout with a shell. Give it the rubric, a base..head range, the exact changed-path list, and spec/task paths, and it fetches what it needs at whatever depth each hunk warrants. Two corollaries are load-bearing: a prompt-payload fitter is evidence the payload is wrong, and prior findings come from session continuity rather than a re-render.",
        "taskIds": [],
        "title": "A reviewer is an executor: pass identities, not payloads"
      },
      {
        "files": [],
        "kind": "step",
        "ordinal": 3,
        "rIds": [
          "R1"
        ],
        "sourceRefs": [
          "spec",
          "rid-R1",
          "task-1"
        ],
        "summary": "codex resume silently dropped the sandbox mode, the reasoning effort, and --skip-git-repo-check, then fell through to a fresh session on failure. Resume now carries the same guarantees as a fresh dispatch and fails loudly. Proven live: a resumed header shows read-only and xhigh, across processes and after a >10 minute gap.",
        "taskIds": [
          "fn-169-review-subsystem-agentic-first-pass.1"
        ],
        "title": "Make resume trustworthy before relying on it"
      },
      {
        "files": [],
        "kind": "step",
        "ordinal": 4,
        "rIds": [
          "R2"
        ],
        "sourceRefs": [
          "spec",
          "rid-R2",
          "task-3"
        ],
        "summary": "Phase 1 resumes with the contract and the machine-read reply grammar but no re-rendered findings. Phase 2 fires only on a surfaced resume failure, rebuilding the prompt with them. The order cannot be collapsed: a lean prompt reaching a context-free session is a fresh blind review with the priors dropped. codex only; host has no session by design and always injects.",
        "taskIds": [
          "fn-169-review-subsystem-agentic-first-pass.3"
        ],
        "title": "Resume carries a lean prompt; injection is the fallback"
      },
